Phase 1 Report

Initial Forensics Project Report  
Kali Linux Setup, Tools, and GitHub Version Control

Overview

This project focuses on building the foundation for a digital forensics lab using Kali Linux. The goal was to set up a working forensic environment, identify the tools needed for investigations, and learn how to properly track and store work using GitHub version control.

Kali Linux Setup Plan

I chose to install Kali Linux locally using VMware Workstation on my personal computer. I went with a local virtual machine instead of a cloud option because it gives me more direct control over the system, especially when working with disk images, memory dumps, and network traffic in later phases.

Setup Steps Included:
- Enabling virtualization in BIOS
- Downloading the Kali Linux ISO
- Creating a virtual machine in VMware
- Allocating CPU, RAM, and storage resources
- Installing Kali Linux and updating system packages

After installation, I updated all packages and installed essential forensic tools to prepare the system for investigation work.

Forensic Tools Selected

To prepare for digital forensic tasks, I grouped tools into categories based on their function:

Disk Forensics
- Autopsy – used for analyzing file systems and recovering deleted files  
- Sleuth Kit – command-line tools for deeper file system investigation  

Memory Forensics
- Volatility 3 – used to analyze RAM and identify running processes or hidden malware  
- WinPMEM – used to capture memory from Windows systems  

Network Forensics
- Wireshark – used to inspect network packets and traffic patterns  
- tcpdump – used to capture network traffic from the command line  

Why These Tools
These tools were chosen because they cover the three core areas of forensics: disk, memory, and network analysis. Together, they allow a complete investigation of system activity and potential compromises.

Evidence Strategy

The plan for handling digital evidence is based on standard forensic practices:

- Create bit-by-bit disk images instead of modifying original data  
- Capture memory while the system is running to preserve live activity  
- Record network traffic during simulated events  
- Use hashing (SHA256) to verify data integrity  
- Maintain chain of custody for all collected evidence  

This ensures that any evidence collected remains reliable and defensible.

GitHub and Version Control

To track all project work, I used GitHub for version control.

Steps Completed:
- Created a GitHub account and repository named `Forensics-Kali-Project`  
- Installed Git on my system  
- Initialized a local repository for the project files  
- Added the Initial Proposal document  
- Made a clear initial commit  
- Pushed the project to GitHub  

This setup allows all changes to be tracked over time and ensures that my work is properly documented and easy to review.

Conclusion

This phase focused on building the foundation for a forensic investigation environment. I successfully set up Kali Linux, selected key forensic tools, and implemented version control using GitHub.

At this stage, I am prepared to move forward into hands-on forensic work, including evidence acquisition and analysis in the next phases of the project.
